<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emmendingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emmendingen is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, north of Freiburg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a>. Near it stand the ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochburg">Hochburg</a>, the castle of the margraves of Hachberg, and the town was later an administrative seat of the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. Goethe's sister Cornelia lived there and is buried in the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emmendingen.jpg" alt="Emmendingen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas Schwarzkopf · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rathaus_in_Emmendingen,_erbaut_1989-92.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Emmendingen lies on the Elz river, 14 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a> between the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> and the Kaiserstuhl. The land is between 189 and 368 metres high. The Elz, flowing on the western edge of the town, divides near Riegel into the Old Elz and the Leopoldskanal, which was built in the middle of the 19th century. The nearby Kaiserstuhl, a range of volcanic hills, is considered one of the warmest places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and is a wine-growing area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first mention of the town in a document dates to 1091, though it is much older. It was the seat of the counts of Hochberg, a cadet branch of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. It received market rights from the emperor in 1418, and in 1590 Margrave Jacob III raised it to the status of a town and walled it. A neighbouring town, Nieder-Emmendingen, merged with it in 1883. The coat of arms shows the red bend on gold of the old arms of Baden, the symbol of the local lord, and a knight; seals earlier showed a ploughshare instead of the knight, and the present form was adopted in 1960.</p><p>The post was first established in 1745 as a Posthalterei. The Goethe Gymnasium was converted from a Latin School in 1667. The author Johann Georg Schlosser, a brother-in-law of Goethe, served for some years as Oberamtmann, the bailiff. His wife Cornelia, born Cornelia Friederica Christiana Goethe, was the only sibling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a>, and they lived in a house on the main square which is now the municipal library.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Emmendingen is the capital of the district of the same name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> and has more than 26,000 residents. It lies on the Baden Main Line between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> and Basel. The old town hall on the main square now houses the German Diary Archive, the only archive of diaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. A Jewish Museum stands on Castle Square, and a Jewish school offering courses on Jewish culture and history has been run since 2006 by the Jewish congregation together with an association for Jewish history and culture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emmendingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1190 the lands of Margrave Hermann IV of Baden were divided, and his son Henry received the castle of Hachberg, the Hochburg near <b>Emmendingen</b>, with lands in the Breisgau.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-hachberg">Margraviate of Hachberg</a></li></ul></section>
